Looking through the source it would appear that this is the validation logic that is generated:

```
func (m *ConfigCreate) validateData(formats strfmt.Registry) error {

	for k := range m.Data {

		if err := validate.Required("data"+"."+k, "body", m.Data[k]); err != nil {
			return err
		}

		if err := validate.Required("data"+"."+k, "body", m.Data[k]); err != nil {
			return err
		}

	}

	return nil
}
```

...which is weird because the two `if` statements are the same check. Secondly, it appears that if any of the property values in the map are the zero value (i.e. a bool property set to false) it causes the required check to fail.

It appears that the required state of the `data` property is erroneously making all properties 1) required and 2) must be non-zero values.

Removing `x-nullable: false`  doesn't change the `validateData()` function logic.

issue.body:
## Problem statement

If a property is defined as a pure map (without any other fixed fields), making it "required" does not work in generated code.

## Swagger specification

Consider this object:

```
"properties": {
    "currencies": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": {
        "$ref": "#/definitions/amount"
      }
    },
},
"required": ["currency"]
```

This produces (correctly) a go representation of `map[string]Currency`. It seems however that the `required` directive is ignored, requests that do not include `currencies` are accepted and defaulted to an empty map.

I want them to be rejected as per the json-spec.
